<article class="env-lemma" id="09Q0">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09Q0">Lemma <span data-tag="09Q0">23.8.2</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a regular ring. Let $\mathfrak p \subset R$ be a prime. Let $f_1, \ldots , f_ r \in \mathfrak p$ be a regular sequence. Then the completion of </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  A = (R/(f_1, \ldots , f_ r))_\mathfrak p = R_\mathfrak p/(f_1, \ldots , f_ r)R_\mathfrak p  \]
</div>
  <p> is a complete intersection in the sense defined above. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The completion of $A$ is equal to $A^\wedge = R_\mathfrak p^\wedge /(f_1, \ldots , f_ r)R_\mathfrak p^\wedge $ because completion for finite modules over the Noetherian ring $R_\mathfrak p$ is exact (Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00MA" data-tag="00MA">10.97.1</a>). The image of the sequence $f_1, \ldots , f_ r$ in $R_\mathfrak p$ is a regular sequence by Algebra, Lemmas <a href="/tag/00MB" data-tag="00MB">10.97.2</a> and <a href="/tag/00LM" data-tag="00LM">10.68.5</a>. Moreover, $R_\mathfrak p^\wedge $ is a regular local ring by More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/07NY" data-tag="07NY">15.44.4</a>. Hence the result holds by our definition of complete intersection for complete local rings. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>